NAME
====

**wattle** - run Wattle programs and a Wattle REPL

SYNOPSIS
========

**wattle** [**-m** _path_] [**run**] [_options_] [_script_ [_args_ ...]]
**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **check** [_options_] [_script_]
**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **build img** [**-l** _module_ ...] _source_ [_output_]
**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **twig** _verb_ [_argument_]
**wattle** **help** [_subcommand_ ...]
**wattle** [**-h** | **-v**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**wattle** runs programs written in Wattle, a Lisp-like programming language.
Wattle reimplements the virtual machine, compiler and core library from the
Janet programming language in Zig with a syntax inspired by Clojure.

**wattle** has four subcommands. **run** runs a script, evaluates code or
starts a REPL (read-eval-print loop). **check** compiles a script without
running it. **build** makes an image from source. **twig** manages installed
packages. **help** describes a subcommand.

**run** is the subcommand when no other is named. Where **wattle** would
otherwise report an unrecognised subcommand or option, or no subcommand, it
reads the rest of the command line as the arguments of **run**. The command
lines ``wattle``, ``wattle hello.wattle`` and ``wattle -e '(+ 1 2)'`` are
therefore the same as ``wattle run``, ``wattle run hello.wattle`` and
``wattle run -e '(+ 1 2)'``. A mistyped subcommand is read as the name of a
script, and a script named after a subcommand is run with ``wattle run``
in front of it.

**-h**, **-v** and **-m** are read only before the subcommand name. A
subcommand does not read them again.

An option that takes a value can be written ``--eval=code``, and short options
can be combined, as in ``-qr``. ``--`` stops the processing of options.

An unrecognised option, an option missing its value or a missing parameter
prints a message and a pointer to **--help** on standard error and exits with
status 1.

**wattle** exits when the script and any tasks it scheduled on the event loop
have finished.

Options
-------

- **-h**, **--help**:
  Print a usage summary and exit. Each subcommand also reads **-h**, and
  prints its own usage.

- **-v**, **--version**:
  Print the version of Wattle and exit.

- **-m**, **--syspath** _path_:
  Set ``*syspath*``, the directory system modules are loaded from, to _path_.
  This replaces the value taken from `WATTLE_PATH`.

run
---

**wattle** [**-m** _path_] [**run**] [_options_] [_script_ [_args_ ...]]

Options are read until the first argument that does not begin with ``-``.
That argument is the _script_, and every argument after it is passed to the
script without being read as an option. Once the script has run, **wattle**
calls the ``main`` function it defines, if any, with the script's path
followed by _args_. The same list is bound to ``*args*``. With no _script_,
and no **-e**, **run** starts a REPL.

An error that a _script_, a module loaded with **-l** or code given to
**-e** does not handle ends the process with status 1.

The options are applied in a fixed order, whatever order they are written in:
first the settings (**-d**, **-c**, **-C**, **-s**, **-r**, **-q** and
**-i**), then **-l**, then **-e**, and last the _script_. A setting therefore
applies to every library, expression and script, and **-l** runs before every
**-e**. Several **-e** options and several **-l** options run in the order
given.

- **-e**, **--eval** _code_:
  Evaluate _code_ as Wattle source. The REPL is not started afterwards unless
  **-r** is also given.

- **-l**, **--lib** _module_:
  Import _module_ into the environment before continuing, without a prefix, so
  its bindings are available to later options, the _script_ and the REPL.

- **-i**, **--img**:
  Treat _script_ as an image made by **build img** rather than as source, load
  it and call its ``main`` function.

- **-r**, **--repl**:
  Start the REPL after the _script_ or the other options have run.

- **-s**, **--stdin**:
  Read REPL input as raw lines from standard input and write prompts to
  standard output. Implies **-r**.

- **-d**, **--debug**:
  Set ``*debug*`` and ``*redef*``. An error in the REPL enters the debugger,
  and a top-level definition can be rebound.

- **-q**, **--quiet**:
  Do not print the banner when the REPL starts.

- **-c**, **--color**:
  Print values, errors and documentation with ANSI colour, and highlight the
  source typed at the REPL. If **-C** is also given, **-C** applies.

- **-C**, **--no-color**:
  Print values, errors and documentation without ANSI colour, and do not
  highlight the source typed at the REPL.

check
-----

**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **check** [_options_] [_script_]

Compile _script_ without running it and report every error. With no _script_,
check standard input instead. The exit status is 0 after errors are reported,
unless **-b** is given.

- **-e**, **--lint-error** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message stops compilation with an error.
  _level_ is one of ``none``, ``relaxed``, ``normal``, ``strict`` or ``all``,
  written without a leading colon, or a number. The default is ``none``.

- **-w**, **--lint-warn** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message is printed as a warning. _level_ takes
  the same values as for **-e**. The default is ``normal``.

- **-b**, **--bail**:
  Stop at the first error in the source and exit with status 1.

build
-----

**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **build img** [**-l** _module_ ...] _source_ [_output_]

Load the module at _source_ and write an image of its environment to
_output_. If _output_ is omitted, it is _source_ with ``.wattle`` replaced by
``.jimage``. **run -i** loads the image.

- **-l**, **--lib** _module_:
  Import _module_ into the environment before _source_ is loaded, as **run**
  does. Several **-l** options run in the order given.

Other targets are not yet available.

twig
----

**wattle** [**-m** _path_] **twig** _verb_ [_argument_]

Manage the packages installed under ``*syspath*``. A build made without the
OS library does not include **twig**, and each verb exits with status 1 after
saying so.

- **install** _directory_:
  Install the package in _directory_.

- **reinstall** _name_:
  Reinstall the installed package _name_.

- **uninstall** _name_:
  Uninstall the package _name_.

- **update**:
  Reinstall every installed package.

- **clean**:
  Uninstall every orphaned package: one marked for automatic removal that no
  other package depends on.

- **list**:
  List the installed packages.

help
----

**wattle help** [_subcommand_ ...]

Print the usage of the last _subcommand_ named. ``wattle help twig install``
prints the usage of **twig install**. With no _subcommand_, **help** reports
an error.

REPL
====

The REPL reads a line at a time and evaluates each form once it is complete.
The prompt shows the line number and any open delimiters. Documentation for a
binding is shown with ``(doc symbol)``, and ``(doc)`` lists the bindings in
scope.

Prompts are written to standard error, or to standard output with **-s**.

ENVIRONMENT
===========

- `WATTLE_PATH`:
  A list of directories to load system modules from, separated by ``:``, or by
  ``;`` on Windows. The last directory becomes ``*syspath*`` and is searched
  first. If unset, ``*syspath*`` is `/usr/local/lib/wattle`.

- `WATTLE_HISTFILE`:
  A file to keep the REPL's history in. Each form submitted at the REPL on a
  terminal is appended to it, and the forms in it are recalled with the Up
  key in a later session. If unset, history lasts only until the REPL exits.

- `NO_COLOR`:
  If set to a non-empty value, disable colour. Colour is otherwise used when
  both standard output and standard error are terminals.

An environment variable set to the empty string is treated as unset.

EXIT STATUS
===========

**wattle** exits `0` on success and `1` if a script, a subcommand or an option
raises an error that is not handled. An error in the REPL is printed and does not end the
session. A program can choose its own status with ``(os/exit status)``.

EXAMPLES
========

Start the REPL:

````
$ wattle
````

Run a script with two arguments:

````
$ wattle hello.wattle Alice Bob
````

Evaluate an expression and print its result:

````
$ wattle -e '(print (+ 1 2 3))'
````

Compile a program into an image and run the image:

````
$ wattle build img hello.wattle hello.jimage
$ wattle run -i hello.jimage Alice
````

Check a script for compile errors without running it, stopping at the first:

````
$ wattle check --bail hello.wattle
````

Install a package and list the installed packages:

````
$ wattle twig install ./my-twig
$ wattle twig list
````

SEE ALSO
========

Wattle does not yet have a written manual. The core library documents itself:
``(doc symbol)`` prints a binding's documentation in the REPL and ``(doc)``
lists the bindings in scope. Example programs are distributed with the source
at <https://github.com/pyrmont/wattle>.

HISTORY
=======

Wattle began as a fork of Janet, the language created by Calvin Rose. It has
its own version numbering: the binding ``wattle/version`` reports that
version.

